#!/usr/bin/env bash
# Standing engineering directives — injected only on build/plan prompts (token-thrifty; silent otherwise).
# Keyword-gated against the payload with path-bearing fields (cwd, transcript_path) stripped first,
# so a keyword in a directory name (e.g. ~/design-system) can't false-trigger on every prompt.
# What remains is the prompt + ids — a match means the verb is in the user's prompt. No JSON parser dependency.
input="$(LC_ALL=C sed -E 's/"(transcript_path|cwd)"[[:space:]]*:[[:space:]]*"[^"]*"//g')"
if printf '%s' "$input" | LC_ALL=C grep -qiE '\b(implement|build|refactor|plan|feature|fix|add|migrat[a-z]*|design|tdd|task)\b'; then
  cat <<'DIRECTIVES'
<engineering-discipline>
- PLAN first, hunting known AND unknown unknowns before writing code. For substantial or behavior-touching work, adversarially critique the plan/diff with a critic subagent, matched to the risk: a sonnet critic at low/standard effort for most changes, opus at higher effort for architecture or subtle-correctness work, and the costly fable/codex critics only for the highest-risk or shippable work. Skip the critic pass on trivial or mechanical edits.
- TDD: write the failing test first, confirm it fails for the right reason, then the minimal green.
- Pure functions; push I/O, state mutation, and side effects to the edges.
- Craft the developer experience: APIs and CLIs should be a joy to use — clear names, sensible defaults, helpful errors, and the ecosystem's own idioms and conventions (least-surprise), so calling your code feels natural, even fun.
- No god classes or functions: one responsibility each, keep them small; split anything that sprawls.
- MEASURE complexity on the functions you added, before you push, and split anything over ~10. Use the linter already installed rather than a new tool, scoped to the changed files and passed on the command line so nothing is added to the repo config: `git diff --name-only <base>..HEAD | grep -E '\.tsx?$' | tr '\n' '\0' | xargs -0 pnpm exec eslint --rule '{"complexity":["warn",8]}'` (Python: `ruff check --select C901 --config 'lint.mccabe.max-complexity=8'`). Do NOT switch the rule on repo-wide: a codebase that predates the rule will have violations everywhere, and a gate that is red on a clean tree gets ignored. Judge your own additions; leave the existing ones alone unless asked.
- Cost-aware delegation: sonnet for mechanical/low-risk work, opus for architecture/deep debugging; set reasoning effort to the minimum that fits the task.
- Comment, prose, doc, and naming cleanup goes to a SONNET subagent at LOW effort — never opus, and never the main loop. The judgement is per-line and local ("does deleting this let someone make a wrong change?"), so the work is bounded by how many files you read, not by how hard the thinking is. Give the agent the rule, the file list, and a verify command; fan out one agent per worktree or per directory so they are file-disjoint and run in parallel.
- Structure every plan as FILE-DISJOINT units so multiple subagents can execute in parallel with no collision.
- Comment ONLY when most needed. The default is no comment. Write one only when its absence would let a competent reader make a WRONG change — a trap, or a why the code cannot state. Before adding one, ask "what wrong edit does this prevent?"; no answer, no comment. Never comment to justify a decision you just made or to narrate a refactor you just did — that belongs in the PR body, not the source. Calibrate to the density of the repo you are in, not to a general style — read a neighbouring file and match it.
- Match the file's LOCAL comment idiom, not a general style. In a schema, config, or enum where the existing comments are short trailing notes, yours must be short trailing notes too — a comment that wraps onto a second line, or sits above a field its neighbours annotate inline, breaks column alignment and reads as imported-from-elsewhere. Read the nearest ten lines and copy their shape before writing one.
- When you replace an existing mechanism, list the guarantees the old one had and confirm each survives — a rewrite reads as neutral in review while quietly dropping one. Real case: a build script that emitted EXACT resolved versions from node_modules was rewritten to copy the root package.json's `^` ranges, so production DDL would have run under an unpinned CLI. The deleted code's own comment said so; the rewrite deleted the comment with it. Comments on the code you are replacing are a checklist, not noise.
- Check CODEOWNERS before touching a file. Deleting or restructuring infrastructure another team owns (a CI job, a container stage, a deploy path) is their decision, not a detail of your feature — even when your change makes it redundant. Raise it as a separate PR or a ticket, and never let it ride along inside a feature commit where their review is a rubber stamp on a diff about something else.
- Size a commit to ONE block of work, not to one PR. A branch that adds a feature plus its build change plus a seed rewrite is three commits — a reviewer should be able to approve the tooling move without re-reading the feature. Split by concern; if you cannot name a commit without "and", it is two commits.
- Do NOT annotate, document, or tidy code the branch did not introduce. A pre-existing model, doc, or migration that your change made stale is a note for the PR body or a follow-up ticket; editing it widens the diff a reviewer must re-read and is the same out-of-scope violation as any other. Repairing drift your own change caused in your own new code is the only exception.
- Typographic tells give AI authorship away and read as unedited output: the section sign, decorative box-drawing banners, and emoji headings. Write "section 7", not the sign; use a plain heading, not a rule of dashes.
- Reuse before you write: if a function is reasonably reusable, reuse it. Before adding a helper, `git grep` for one that already does the job — by name AND by a distinctive line of its body, since the copy you are about to make may exist under a different name with renamed parameters. Extend the existing one rather than adding a near-twin, and say which one you checked.
- When one commit adds two files in the same directory, diff them against each other before finishing. The general reuse rule above did not stop `formatDose` being written twice inside a single commit; this check would have.
- When you add a field to a mock or context object literal, grep for every sibling file with the same literal and update its TYPE in the same edit. A literal and its type are two sources of truth for one shape, and typecheck stays green while they disagree.
- Before any push, write a short "what this diff assumes" note from a read of the FINAL diff: the invariants it relies on and what would break them. The test is whether it can be written at all — if it cannot, the code is not understood yet and is not ready to ship.
- Verify a "no callers anywhere" claim in EVERY worktree/branch of the stack, not just the one you are in. A grep scoped to one worktree that gets generalised to "the repo" is how a still-used helper gets deleted.
- NEVER conclude anything from a diff line count without re-running it with `-w`. Wrapping a block in a function, a prop, or a try/catch re-indents every line inside it, and the raw stat reports that as a rewrite. A 70/75 "this rewrote pre-existing code" finding measured 9/14 under `git diff -w` — the opposite conclusion. Quote the `-w` number, not the raw one.
- To decide whether a line is pre-existing or yours, use `git blame -l <base>` and test whether the owning commit is in `<upstream>..<base>`. Matching line CONTENT false-positives on `}`/`});` and false-negatives when your own addition happens to equal a pre-existing line.
- An audit that has to produce a finding will produce one. When a metric hands you the violation you went looking for, re-derive it a second way BEFORE writing it up — and if the second method disagrees, the metric was the artifact, not the finding.
</engineering-discipline>
DIRECTIVES
fi
exit 0
